A||N||11||MikeSchaefer||Stumbling 2.8 when given gas||||4||
Z||000000||MikeSchaefer||02-20-2001||07:57 PM||||Hi, my Fiero has been having a weird problem lately.  When I give it gas, it kind of stumbles and then kicks in, there is about a 2 second lag before I feel the car go.  When I press in the pedal, it just kind of stumbles and I hear some loud popping sound from the exhaust.  Anyone heard of this kind of problem before, my father thinks the cat needs to be adjusted, but I have a feeling it's something else.  It does this in every gear, once it warms up it does it a lot less.  It can cruise fine, my gas mileage might be down lately, but I'm not 100% sure about that.  Has been doing this for a couple of months now.<BR>Thanks in advance,<BR>Mike Schaefer <A HREF="http://surf.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://surf.to/fiero</A> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||02-20-2001||09:25 PM||||Well, you can't 'adjust' the cat. Check the timming, give it a tune up, clean the idle air control valve.||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||02-21-2001||06:42 PM||||just going to state the obvious, have you checked the timing? I know that sometimes will cause a slight hesitation. It would probably explain why it does it less after it warms up since the ECM adjusts the timing once it's in a closed loop (sensors at operating temperature). Until that point it depends mostly on the static timing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||MikeSchaefer||02-21-2001||10:16 PM||||Hi, thanks for the replies.  I thought that I had just had my timing adjusted, but I'll have my mechanic take a look again.  Is that what it probably is?  Would a clogged cat cause these same symptoms or is there a difference?<BR>Thanks again,<BR>Mike Schaefer||||reg||1
Z||000004||GarethWright||02-22-2001||02:42 AM||||My '87 GT is doing the EXACT same thing, pops hesitation and all, also getting a reliable 14.5 MPG.  <BR>I have a new cat on it, made very little or no difference, except the new cat doesnt rattle like the old one did.  <BR>Lemme know what your mechanic said about the timing.  I thought that as the ECM controls timing, we can't adjust it, or is it more a matter of a new chain?<BR>Good Luck, maybe we can both get this fixed.<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000005||HAGS||02-22-2001||09:12 AM||||I've had that problem, no telling what it could be though since everything was broken on my car...happend real bad if it got too hot though, detonation...then a little speed cooled it down and it ran fine, never did find the coolant leak but now that the engine is blown I have all the time in the world...||||reg||1
Z||000006||fieroparts.com||02-22-2001||09:32 AM||||Try the TPS more then not, this is a good place to start.||||reg||14
Z||000007||filthyscarecrow||02-23-2001||01:52 AM||||check your plugs, wires, distrubutor cap and rotor. sounds like the spark is blowing out at full throttle. i had this problem once, new cap and rotor solved it. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||filthyscarecrow||02-23-2001||01:52 AM||||check your plugs, wires, distrubutor cap and rotor. sounds like the spark is blowing out at full throttle. i had this problem once, new cap and rotor solved it. <P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||02-23-2001||07:21 AM||||A clogged Catalytic convertor would cause more problems at high RPMs than at low RPMs.  Your problem is more likely the throttle position sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000010||GarethWright||02-27-2001||03:22 AM||||Well, forked over the cash for a new TPS today, should be in tomorrow or wednesday, I'll let ya know||||reg||1
Z||000011||MikeSchaefer||02-27-2001||10:24 AM||||alright!  What did that sensor cost you?<BR>Thanks,<BR>Mike||||reg||14
